Vitrin OS — licensing notice

This file is the normative path -> license map for this repository.

Decision D-005 (docs/plan/20-decision-log.md) settles the split; D-016 in the
same log records its execution — why MPL-2.0 was taken over the LGPL-3.0
fallback D-005 also recorded, and exactly where the line between the
copyleft and permissive halves falls. D-015 records the related decision to
file no patents and rely on defensive publication plus the license patent
grants instead.

Where a source file carries an `SPDX-License-Identifier:` header, that
header is authoritative for that file. This map exists so a reader can see
the shape of the whole tree without grepping it, and so the boundaries are
stated in one place rather than inferred.


APACHE-2.0 (LICENSE) — explicit patent grant

  - the protocol itself: protocol/vitrin-v0.xml, protocol/vitrin-v0.rng;
  - everything transliterated from it: crates/vitrin-protocol (its
    checked-in generated bindings *and* the hand-written wire/fixed-point/
    error code around them) and shim/include/vitrin-protocol.h, the C
    header emitted from the same IDL by the same scanner;
  - the code generator and its driver: crates/vitrin-scanner, crates/xtask
    — a third party writing a Go, TypeScript or C++ SDK forks these;
  - the conformance and robustness instruments: crates/vitrin-golden,
    crates/vitrin-mock-shim, crates/vitrin-realm-init-fixtures,
    crates/vitrin-skip, fuzz/. crates/vitrin-skip is the sanctioned test
    skip (issue #288): it measures whether a suite actually ran, is never
    linked into vitrind or installed, and had to sit on this side of the
    split for a second reason as well -- crates/xtask imports its marker
    constant so the emitters and the census cannot desynchronise, and
    Apache-2.0 xtask must not take that from an MPL-2.0 crate. The
    last of those holds deliberately broken vitrin-realm-init substitutes,
    each defeating exactly one of the core's confinement checkpoints so a
    test can prove that checkpoint fires; they are instruments, never
    installed and never shipped, so they sit with the other instruments
    rather than with the MPL-2.0 helper they stand in for;
  - crates/vitrin-png — a hand-rolled, zero-dependency, ENCODE-ONLY PNG
    writer. It was vitrin-golden/src/png.rs and stays on this side of the
    line although the MPL-2.0 core now links it (WS-E.2.4, issue #216): it
    was written as an instrument, a third party writing a harness of their
    own should be able to take it, and an MPL-2.0 crate linking an
    Apache-2.0 one is the direction that works. What it must never grow is
    a decoder; see the crate's own docs for why that absence is the
    reviewable property;
  - the client SDKs: sdk/python/ and any future SDK;
  - the integration harness, the per-kernel isolation harness and the shipped
    examples: tests/integration/, tests/kernel-matrix/, examples/. The
    kernel-matrix directory holds a shell collector, a static C init that is
    PID 1 in a throwaway QEMU guest, and the boot rows they produce; none of
    it links into any shipped binary and all of it is a measurement
    instrument, so it sits with the other conformance instruments;
  - packaging/ -- the artifacts a distributor installs on a host rather than
    links into a binary. Today that is packaging/apparmor/vitrind, the
    AppArmor profile issue #286 needs on Ubuntu 24.04+. It is named here
    EXPLICITLY rather than left to the catch-all below, because the catch-all
    covers "repository tooling that is not part of a shipped artifact" and an
    installed AppArmor profile is exactly a shipped artifact -- it lands in
    /etc/apparmor.d/ on an operator's machine. Apache-2.0 for the same reason
    the SDKs are: a distributor packaging this project, or a fork wanting a
    profile for their own binary, must not have to touch a copyleft file to
    do it. The profile carries an inline `# SPDX-License-Identifier:
    Apache-2.0` header of its own.

  The rule behind that list: content derived from the Apache-2.0 protocol
  artifacts stays Apache-2.0. Nobody should have to touch a copyleft file to
  write a client, an alternate compositor, or an integration.

